@document.meta
title: juck specification
description: Syntax and core semantics of juck
created: 2026-10-04
version: 0.1
@end

* juck
  juck is a statically typed, Lisp-like language whose syntax is JSON.
  Code is data: a program is a JSON value, so other languages can load,
  modify and re-emit it with an ordinary JSON parser.

  @code d2
  direction: right
  source: ".juck source"
  tree: "value tree"
  ast: AST
  json: "--json"
  other: "other languages"
  source -> tree: reader
  tree -> ast: analyzer
  ast -> eval
  tree -> json
  json -> other
  other -> source: re-emit
  #color re-emit green
  @end

* Source syntax
  Source files (`.juck`) are JSONC: JSON plus

  - `//` line comments and `/* */` block comments
  - trailing commas in arrays and objects (`[1, 2,]`, `{"a": 1,}`)

  A file holds exactly one value, like a JSON document: an array whose
  elements are the program's top-level forms.

  @code json
  [
    ["def", {"name": "pi", "type": "f64"}, 3.14159],
    ["print", "pi"],
  ]
  @end

  Comments are discarded by the reader. Anything that must survive a
  round trip through other tools (like documentation) belongs in the
  code as data, for example a `"doc"` key in a definition.

  Objects must not contain duplicate keys; the reader rejects them.

* Interchange format
  `juck <file> --json` emits strict JSON (no comments, no trailing
  commas) that any JSON parser accepts:

  @code json
  {"juck": "0.1", "program": [ ... ]}
  @end

  `"juck"` is the format version; it changes when the shape of code
  changes. Compiled programs embed the same JSON.

  Anything whose order matters is written as an array, never as object
  keys: JSON does not guarantee key order, and some languages' parsers
  do not preserve it.

* Values in code
  How each kind of JSON value is read as code:

  @code d2
  #color symbol blue
  #color error red
  direction: right
  value: "value in code"
  value -> "number: literal"
  value -> "true/false/null: literal"
  value -> "string: symbol"
  value -> object
  value -> array
  object -> "str key: string"
  object -> "other: error"
  array -> "special form"
  array -> "function call"
  @end

  - /number/: a numeric literal. Integers (no `.`, `e` or `E`) are `i64`,
    everything else is `f64`.
  - `true`, `false`: boolean literals.
  - `null`: the null value.
  - /string/: a *symbol*, meaning a name: a variable, a function, a type
    or a special form. Strings are never literal text in code.
  - /array/: a form. The first element is the head; see {* Forms}.
  - /object/: a literal object; see {* Literal objects}.

** Names
   A symbol may not contain `.` (see {* Constructing and accessing values})
   or start with `@` (see {** Intrinsics}), and can't be the name of a
   special form.

   Names can't shadow: a local name (a `let` binding or a parameter) can't
   reuse the name of a built-in, a global defined anywhere in the program,
   or another local that is still in scope. Scopes that don't overlap may
   reuse a name.

   @code d2
   #color error red
   #color ok green
   direction: right
   binding: "local name"
   binding -> "builtin: error"
   binding -> "global: error"
   binding -> "local in scope: error"
   binding -> "anything else: ok"
   @end

* Literal objects
  An object in expression position must have exactly one key, and the
  key says what kind of literal it is.

  - `{"str": "text"}`: a string literal.

  Any other object in expression position is an error. Objects also
  appear inside special forms (like the definition object of `fn`),
  where the form defines what keys mean, and as type expressions; see
  {* Types}.

* Forms
  A form is an array `[head, args...]`. What happens depends on the head:

  @code d2
  direction: right
  head: "form head"
  head -> "special form: own rules"
  head -> "intrinsic: compile time"
  head -> "function: runtime call"
  @end

  - A /special form/ has its own evaluation rules; see {** Special forms}.
  - An /intrinsic/ is called like a function, but the compiler handles
    it; see {** Intrinsics}.
  - Anything else is a /function call/: the head and the arguments are
    evaluated left to right, then the function is applied to the
    arguments. The head may be any expression, so
    `[["make-adder", 1], 5]` calls the function `make-adder` returns.

  Everything that can be an ordinary function is one, and lives in the
  standard library rather than in this spec.

** Special forms
   Special forms are the forms that can't be functions: they bind names,
   define types, or control whether and when their arguments are
   evaluated. Their names are reserved and cannot be redefined:
   `def`, `fn`, `lambda`, `type`, `if`, `let`, `do`, `data`, `template`,
   `insert`, `splice`.

*** def
    Defines a global name. Only allowed at the top level.

    @code json
    ["def", {"name": "pi", "type": "f64"}, 3.14159]
    @end

    The definition object has a `"name"` and a `"type"`, both required.

*** fn
    Defines a named function. Only allowed at the top level; for a local
    function, bind a `lambda` with `let`.

    @code json
    ["fn", {"name": "add", "params": [["a", "i64"], ["b", "i64"]], "returns": "i64"},
      ["+", "a", "b"]]
    @end

    @code d2
    #color optional yellow
    direction: right
    form: "fn form"
    form -> fn: "1st"
    form -> "definition object": "2nd"
    form -> body: "rest"
    "definition object" -> name
    "definition object" -> params
    "definition object" -> returns
    "definition object" -> "doc (optional)"
    @end

    Definition object keys:
    - `"name"` (required): the function's name.
    - `"params"` (required): an array of `[name, type]` pairs, in order.
    - `"returns"` (required): the return type.
    - `"doc"` (optional): a documentation string.

    The body is one or more expressions; the value of the last one is
    the function's result.

*** lambda
    An anonymous function. Same definition object as `fn`, without
    `"name"`.

    @code json
    ["lambda", {"params": [["x", "i64"]], "returns": "i64"}, ["*", "x", "x"]]
    @end

    A lambda can use names from the scope it is defined in; it keeps
    them for as long as it exists (it is a closure).

*** type
    Names a type.

    @code json
    ["type", "Shape", {"union": [
      ["circle", "f64"],
      ["rect", {"struct": [["w", "f64"], ["h", "f64"]]}]
    ]}]
    @end

*** if
    @code json
    ["if", condition, then, else]
    @end

    `condition` must be a `bool`. Both branches are required and must
    have the same type. Only the chosen branch is evaluated.

*** let
    Local bindings, evaluated in order; each binding can see the ones
    before it. Each binding is a `[name, type, value]` triple.

    @code json
    ["let", [["x", "i64", 1], ["y", "i64", ["+", "x", 1]]],
      ["*", "x", "y"]]
    @end

    The body is one or more expressions, as in `fn`.

*** do
    Evaluates expressions in order and returns the value of the last.

    @code json
    ["do", ["print", "x"], ["+", "x", 1]]
    @end

*** data
    Returns its argument as data, unevaluated.

    @code json
    ["data", ["+", 1, 2]]
    @end

    The result has type `data`. Symbols in the argument stay symbols,
    distinct from strings: `["data", "a"]` holds the symbol `a`, while
    `["data", {"str": "a"}]` holds the string "a".

*** template
    Like `data`, but with holes that are filled in when the template is
    evaluated.

    @code json
    ["template", ["+", ["insert", "x"], 1]]
    @end

    - `["insert", expr]` is replaced by the value of `expr`, which must
      be `data`.
    - `["splice", expr]`, as an element of an array, is replaced by the
      elements of `expr`, which must be a `data` array.

    If `x` holds `["*", 2, 3]`, the template above produces
    `["+", ["*", 2, 3], 1]`. Everything else in the template is data, as
    in `data`. `insert` and `splice` are only allowed inside a template,
    and a hole belongs to the outermost template around it.

** Intrinsics
   Intrinsics are called exactly like functions, but the compiler
   handles them, usually because they must run at compile time. Their
   names start with `@`.

   - `["@import", {"str": "path"}]`: loads another module; see
     {* Open questions}.

   Constructors and field access (see {* Constructing and accessing
   values}) are also handled by the compiler, but keep their natural
   syntax.

* Built-ins
  Built-in functions are called like any function, but can't be used as
  values and can't be redefined.

** Arithmetic and comparison
   - `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`: two numbers of the same type (`i64` or `f64`),
     returning that type.
   - `%`: two `i64`, returning `i64`.
   - `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`: two numbers of the same type, returning `bool`.
   - `=`, `!=`: two values of the same type (not functions), returning
     `bool`.
   - `not`: `bool` to `bool`.

   `i64` arithmetic that overflows is a runtime error, and so is
   dividing by zero. `/` truncates toward zero and the result of `%` has
   the sign of the dividend, so `["/", -7, 2]` is `-3` and
   `["%", -7, 3]` is `-1`.

   `f64` arithmetic follows IEEE 754: dividing a non-zero number by zero
   gives positive or negative infinity, and `0.0 / 0.0` gives NaN, which
   is not equal to anything, including itself.

** Output
   - `["print", value]`: writes `value` followed by a newline, and
     returns `null`. `value` must be a built-in type (`i64`, `f64`,
     `bool`, `str`, `null`, `data`), or a struct that defines a `format`
     function returning a built-in type.

   Values are written as JSON: strings in quotes with escapes, data as
   the JSON it is, and floats always with a `.` or an exponent, so they
   read back as `f64`. Floating point division by `0` writes back `null`

** Code as data
   The built-ins for working with `data` are described in
   {* Code as data}.

* Code as data
  A `data` value is code as written: the JSON value exactly, without
  interpretation. Its kinds are null, bool, int, float, symbol (a JSON
  string), array and object. A string literal in code is the object
  `{"str": "..."}`, so code and data have exactly the same shape.

  @code d2
  direction: right
  data: "data value"
  value: "typed value"
  data -> value: eval
  value -> data: convert
  @end

** Inspecting
   - `data-null?`, `data-bool?`, `data-int?`, `data-float?`,
     `data-symbol?`, `data-array?`, `data-object?`: `data` to `bool`.
   - `["data-len", d]`: the number of elements of an array, or of keys
     of an object, as `i64`.
   - `["data-get", d, i]`: element `i` of an array.
   - `["data-slice", d, start, end]`: elements `start` up to (not
     including) `end` of an array, as a new array.
   - `["data-field", d, key]`: the value of a key (a `str`) of an object.
   - `["data-has?", d, key]`: whether an object has a key.

   Using one of these on the wrong kind of data, an index out of bounds,
   or a key that doesn't exist, is a runtime error.

** Converting
   - `data-to-i64`, `data-to-f64`, `data-to-bool`: an int, float or bool
     as a typed value. The kind must match exactly: an int is not
     converted to `f64`.
   - `data-to-str`: the name of a symbol, as a `str`.
   - `["symbol", s]`: the symbol named by the `str` `s`.
   - `["to-data", x]`: the code that evaluates to `x`. Numbers, booleans
     and `null` become themselves, a `str` becomes `{"str": "..."}`, and
     `data` becomes `["data", ...]`. Functions and non-finite floats have
     no code that evaluates to them, so they are an error.

** Building
   - `["data-array", d, ...]`: an array of any number of `data` values.
   - {*** template}: code with holes.

** eval
   `["eval", d]` analyzes, checks and runs `d` as an expression, and
   returns its value as `data`: a `data` result as it is, any other
   result converted with `to-data`.

   - The evaluated code can use globals, but not the local names around
     the `eval`.
   - Only expressions can be evaluated, not `def` or `fn`.
   - Errors in the evaluated code, including type errors, are runtime
     errors of the `eval`.
   - A function result is an error, since it has no `data` form.

* Execution
  A program is evaluated like a Lisp: the top-level forms are evaluated
  in order, from first to last.

  A name defined with `def` or `type` is available to every form after
  it. Functions defined with `fn` at the top level are known to the
  whole program, so a function body may call functions defined later in
  the file, including functions that call each other. Calling a
  function at the top level before its `fn` form has been evaluated is
  an error.

  In the REPL, forms are entered one at a time and evaluated against
  everything entered before. A name can be defined again, but only with
  the same type; functions that use it see the new definition on their
  next call. If a definition's value fails to evaluate, the name keeps
  its previous meaning.

* Constructing and accessing values
  A named type is called like a function to construct a value:

  @code json
  [
    ["type", "Point", {"struct": [["x", "f64"], ["y", "f64"]]}],
    ["Point", 1.0, 2.0]
  ]
  @end

  Arguments are the fields, in declaration order. A union value is
  constructed through its variant:

  @code json
  ["Shape.circle", 2.0]
  @end

  Fields are read with a dot:

  @code json
  ["+", "point.x", "point.y"]
  @end

  A dotted symbol is shorthand for a `.` form, which tools that
  generate code can use directly:

  - `"point.x"` is `[".", "point", "x"]`
  - `["point.scale", 2.0]` is `[[".", "point", "scale"], 2.0]`

* Types
  A type is either a symbol naming a type, or a single-key object
  describing a compound type.

  @code d2
  #color symbol blue
  direction: right
  type -> "symbol: named type"
  type -> struct
  type -> union
  type -> fn
  struct -> "name, type pairs"
  union -> "name, type pairs"
  fn -> "param types, return type"
  @end

** Primitive types
   - `i64`: 64-bit signed integer
   - `f64`: 64-bit float
   - `bool`
   - `str`: string
   - `data`: any juck code as a value, in other words, any JSON value;
     see {* Code as data}
   - `null`: the type of `null`

   There are no implicit conversions: an operation mixing `i64` and
   `f64` is a type error.

   Integers wider than 64 bits are a standard library concern, not
   part of the core language.

** Compound types
   Fields and variants are arrays of `[name, type]` pairs, so their
   order is preserved; it determines memory layout and tag values.

   - `{"struct": [[name, type], ...]}`: a record of named fields.
   - `{"union": [[name, type], ...]}`: a tagged union; a value holds
     exactly one variant, and knows which one.
   - `{"fn": {"params": [type, ...], "returns": type}}`: a function.
     Two function types are equal if their parameter types and return
     types are equal; parameter names are not part of the type.

   Types can be nested, and named with {*** type}:

   @code json
   [
    ["type", "IntOp", {"fn": {"params": ["i64"], "returns": "i64"}}],

    ["fn", {"name": "make-adder", "params": [["n", "i64"]], "returns": "IntOp"},
      ["lambda", {"params": [["x", "i64"]], "returns": "i64"},
        ["+", "x", "n"]]]
   ]
   @end

** Annotations
   For now, every binding is annotated: definitions, function
   parameters and return types, and `let` bindings. Type inference
   will make some of these optional later.

* Open questions
  - ( ) Other compound types: typed lists like `{"list": "i64"}`, and
        maps. (`data` covers code as values; these are for ordinary
        typed collections.)
  - ( ) Type inference, starting with `let` bindings.
  - ( ) Macros: how they're defined, when they're expanded (at analysis
        time), which functions they can call at compile time, and
        generated names (`gensym`) to avoid collisions under the
        no-shadowing rule.
  - ( ) Modules: what `@import` returns, and how a module exposes
        names (probably as an object).
  - ( ) Methods: does `["point.scale", 2.0]` look up a field, or call
        a function with `point` as its first argument (like Zig)?
  - ( ) Matching on union variants: reading which variant a value holds.
  - ( ) Duplicate keys are rejected for now; could they express
        overloading later?
  - ( ) Plain function pointers without captures, for C interop and
        native code.
  - ( ) Defining globals with `eval`, and how that relates to REPL
        redefinition.
